  "response": "## Yes — the repo ships no agent-readable context at all\n\n`crimes_00007` — **Missing Agent Context** (`missing_agent_context`, universal pack), severity `medium`, confidence `0.90`:\n\n> Repo ships no agent-readable instruction files (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`, `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`).\n\nThe evidence lists all four surfaces as absent, plus the aggravating factor:\n\n- no `AGENTS.md` at repo root\n- no `CLAUDE.md` at repo root\n- no `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- no `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- `package.json` declares bin(s): `tsup`, `tsup-node` — **agents have no way to discover commands**\n\nTwo things to know about reading this finding:\n\n1. **The `file` field is not where the fix goes.** It's anchored to `assets/cjs_shims.js` because the schema requires a file, but this is a repo-level finding. The fix lands at the repo root.\n2. **The repo isn't undocumented — it's un-*agent*-documented.** Coverage shows 3 `.md` files exist. Human-facing docs don't clear this; the detector is checking for the specific surfaces an agent loads automatically.\n\n## What this costs you on your first PR\n\nThe scan gives you a concrete picture of what an agent walks into blind here. `missing_agent_context` scores `agent_risk` 0.47 on its own, but its real cost is that it removes the guardrails for everything else in the report:\n\n**You won't know what's load-bearing.** `src/utils.ts` has fan-in 18 and `src/plugin.ts` fan-in 10 (both flagged, `crime_00086` / `crime_00049`), with 29 transitive importers each. Nothing in the repo tells an agent \"these are hubs, prefer additive changes.\" An agent will refactor a helper in `utils.ts` the same way it'd refactor a leaf file.\n\n**You won't know which tests actually protect anything.** There are 12 tests with **zero** `expect`/`assert` calls (`crime_00057`–`crime_00068`) and 14 more using only weak matchers. A green suite here proves the code *ran*. Without an AGENTS.md saying \"the real verification is X,\" an agent will read a passing run as a safety signal it isn't.\n\n**You won't know the entry points.** `src/index.ts` is 459 lines with fan-out 17 and a 293-line `build()` (`crime_00029`); `src/cli-main.ts` has a 153-line `main()`. Two published bins, `tsup` and `tsup-node`, and no stated path from CLI flag → normalization → esbuild/rollup/dts. An agent burns its context window rediscovering that layout on every session — and often reconstructs it wrong.\n\n**You won't know the local idioms.** `getRollupConfig` calls `removeFiles` (`crime_00004` — False Identity); `build` and `buildAll` both mutate. If those names are deliberate legacy, an AGENTS.md says so once. Without it, every agent either \"fixes\" them or trips over them.\n\nThe compounding failure mode: none of these findings are individually severe, but an agent with no repo context treats all 92 as equally actionable and equally safe. Agent context is what turns a finding list into a *priority* list.\n\n## Minimal AGENTS.md that clears the finding\n\nThe detector clears on presence of `AGENTS.md` at the repo root — but a stub that clears the check without carrying real information is worse than nothing, because it silences the signal. Everything below is derived from the scan; the `⚠️` lines need one look at `package.json` before you commit.\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\nBuild tool for TypeScript/JS libraries. Bundles with esbuild, emits\ndeclarations via rollup + api-extractor, exposes the `tsup` and\n`tsup-node` binaries.\n\n## Commands\n\n⚠️ Confirm against package.json scripts before relying on these.\n\n- Install:   `pnpm install`\n- Build:     `pnpm build`\n- Test:      `pnpm test`\n- Typecheck: `pnpm typecheck`\n- Lint:      `pnpm lint`\n\nRun the build and the test suite before proposing a change.\n\n## Layout\n\n| Path                  | Role                                        |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `src/index.ts`        | Public API + `build()` orchestrator         |\n| `src/cli-main.ts`     | CLI arg parsing → `build()`                 |\n| `src/esbuild/`        | JS/CSS bundling; `runEsbuild` is the entry  |\n| `src/rollup.ts`       | `.d.ts` rollup pipeline                     |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts`| Declaration bundling                        |\n| `src/plugin.ts`       | Plugin container + lifecycle hooks          |\n| `src/utils.ts`        | Shared helpers                              |\n| `test/utils.ts`       | Test harness — `run()` builds in a temp dir |\n\n## Load-bearing files — read importers before editing\n\nThese are the repo's top connectivity tier. Prefer additive changes;\ndo not restructure without checking every importer.\n\n- `src/utils.ts`  — 18 direct importers, 29 transitive\n- `src/plugin.ts` — 10 direct importers, 29 transitive\n- `src/log.ts`    —  9 direct importers, 30 transitive\n- `src/index.ts`  — imports 17 modules; changes here fan out widely\n\n## Testing\n\nTests run real builds through `test/utils.ts` `run()` and assert on\nemitted output. Adding a test means adding a fixture + assertions on\nthe generated files.\n\n⚠️ Known gap: a number of existing tests call `run()` without any\nassertions, or assert only with weak matchers. **A passing suite is\nnot sufficient evidence that a change is safe.** Verify new behaviour\nby inspecting emitted output directly.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- `getRollupConfig`, `build`, and `buildAll` perform side effects\n  (file removal, logger construction) despite read-sounding names.\n  This is existing behaviour — do not \"fix\" the names as a drive-by.\n- `*Sync` fs calls in `src/utils.ts` and `src/fs.ts` are deliberate\n  in build-time paths. Don't convert them opportunistically.\n- Plugin hooks (`buildStart`, `buildFinished`, `setup`, `resolveId`)\n  follow esbuild/rollup contracts — signatures are fixed by those APIs.\n\n## Scope rules\n\n- Don't edit `dist/`, `assets/*_shims.js`, or generated `.d.ts` output.\n- Public API changes require a changeset.\n```\n\n**On sizing:** the fix is `effort: small` / `risk: low` per the finding's `add_agent_context` action, and everything above is one file at the repo root. The three `⚠️` markers are the only parts the scan can't source for you — leaving a wrong command in an AGENTS.md is a worse failure than not having one, since agents trust it over their own inspection.",
